rtance is that  china  declared that it supports and it stands  firmly with russia in opposing  the continued eastward expansion of nato  analysts say beijing also wants to cool  sentiment domestically and prevent the  war from stirring emotions among people  despite china claiming to be neutral  over the crisis the widespread online  censorship is telling a different story  voices calling for peace or criticizing  russian president vladimir putin simply  get erased from chinese social media the  playbook china is using makes it  difficult to gauge people's views on  what's happening  but it could have serious negative  effects and even undermine international  efforts to resolve the crisis i think  ultimately to shield people from  the damaging consequences of war  and what is actually happening there on  the ground i think to create apathy in  war is is a really dangerous sentiment  it's going to lead to a more apathetic  generation of people in china for years  to come beijing has yet to refer to the  attack on ukraine as an invasion and  continues to call it a special military  operation and it says its relationship  with russia remains rock solid  and joining me now for more is marika  oldberg she's an expert in china's media  and digital policies and a senior fellow  in the asia program at the think tank  the german marshall fund marika why are  kremlin's talking points on ukraine  being reproduced in chinese state and  social media  um the line that the chinese state has  taken on the war in ukraine  is ultimately that the us and nato are  to blame  and that europe should you know maybe  seek cooperation with russia to to be  rearranged and security architecture in  europe  so this is also  why  chinese official media and chinese  official sources such as you know the  accounts of diplomats  are amplifying these messages coming out  of russia  and like on the one hand you have the  chinese government saying a court  conflict and confrontation are not in  anyone's interests and on the other hand  you have its state media repeating  russian claims about the war  therefore justifying it  why these two opposing strategies  so what i've called this what's  happening here is maximum pro-russian  neutrality so i think what the chinese  government is doing here is leave itself  a little out at the end  because there are some issues also from  the perspective of the chinese public in  terms of you know national sovereignty  which is something  that  the chinese government has put left  right and center in the past and very  clearly this is a problem about the  national sovereignty of ukraine  so it's actually you know there's some  some is facing some some criticism for  that as well but but the basic point  here is that the chinese state wants to  lend as much support as possible to  russia that it can afford  without completely committing itself to  this position so it basically wants to  remain sufficiently  ambiguous  that when things really really go  southwest for russia and things have not  been going well um based on all that we  know right  it can still somehow pull itself out and  say but look we've always we've never  supported russia we've always you know  also given humanitarian aid to ukraine  so really we we haven't been partial in  this conflict even even though they have  been  do you see this media coverage of the  ukraine war somehow setting a precedent  for how russia and china amplify each  other's messages in the future  there's been some amplification in the  past between the two  never super coordinated as far as i know  but definitely some coordination and  then opportunistic amplification  um i do think this is um  slightly more  slightly more  slightly stronger than what we've seen  in the past um it is definitely based on  the the shared assumption that you know  the west is in decline other countries  are on the rise so we might just get  away with this um we know with with  amplifying this and with 